-- title:
--   Existence of level-q automorphisms for γ∈Γ₀(M')
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $q\ge 5$, let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$, let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero and let $\zeta\in L$ be a primitive $q$-th root of unity. Let $H=\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$ be the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, and let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L\subseteq L((X))$ (Laurent series over $L$) which is assumed equal to `laurentBaseChange L` applied to the $q$-expansion function field `xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H`, i.e. the $L$-subfield of $L((X))$ generated by the coefficientwise images under $\mathbb{Q}\to L$ of that subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((X))$. The assertion is that for every $\gamma\in SL(2,\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$ there is an $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H` for $\gamma^{-1}$: for every weight $k\in\mathbb{Z}$, all modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ for the subgroup of $GL(2,\mathbb{R})$ attached to $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$, all $p_f,p_g\in\mathbb{Z}[[X]]$ whose images in $\mathbb{C}[[X]]$ are the $q$-expansions of $f$ and $g$, with $p_g$ nonzero over $\mathbb{Q}$, every $x\in K$ whose image in $L((X))$ is the coefficientwise image of $p_f/p_g$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta)=e^{2\pi i/q}$, one has $\iota_*(\tau x)\cdot q\text{-}\mathrm{exp}(g\mid_k \gamma^{-\sharp}) = q\text{-}\mathrm{exp}(f\mid_k \gamma^{-\sharp})$, where $\delta^{\sharp}=\mathrm{diag}(q,1)^{-1}\delta\,\mathrm{diag}(q,1)$ and $\gamma^{-\sharp}=(\gamma^{-1})^{\sharp}$.
--
--   This is the level-$q$ existence statement for the classical level automorphisms of the function field of $X_H(q^2M')$ (Shimura's theory of the action of $\Gamma_0(M')$ on $q$-expansion fields), formulated over an arbitrary characteristic-zero coefficient field containing $\zeta_q$ and characterised through embeddings $L\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}$ sending $\zeta$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$. It supplies the automorphisms used downstream to identify Galois actions, to compute ramification indices over the Gauss point and to exhibit cyclic inertia in the study of the Jacobian of the full-level modular curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_exists_isLevelAutAt_of_mem_gamma0.lean

